 \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794584646408,"end":1794584648384}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794584648390,"sessionID":"ses_e2a6bf669ffeDZmzRxSZ41RNny","part":{"partID":"prt_1d59492dd001UOiewGfxSpuve5","sessionID":"ses_e2a6bf669ffeDZmzRxSZ41RNny","messageID":"msg_1d5946284001nxj4uvS1NAiJI4","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd5a6ecb4f78d51e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794584646365,"end":1794584648390}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794584648395,"sessionID":"ses_e2a6bf669ffeDZmzRxSZ41RNny","part":{"partID":"prt_1d594968a001UZVI2EXmxdJNWy","sessionID":"ses_e2a6bf669ffeDZmzRxSZ41RNny","messageID":"msg_1d5946284001nxj4uvS1NAiJI4","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8144a4ac5a127711","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\"\n3: description: \"Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reversibility ladder\"\n6:   - \"rollback sentence\"\n7:   - \"stakes read\"\n8:   - \"stop for a yes\"\n9:   - \"irreversible\"\n10:   - \"force push\"\n11:   - \"rewrite history\"\n12:   - \"delete overwrite migrate deploy\"\n13:   - \"sending is publishing\"\n14:   - \"overwriting an untracked file\"\n15:   - \"persistence boundaries\"\n16:   - \"who still speaks the old contract\"\n17:   - \"installing is a mutation\"\n18:   - \"reversible decisions are cheap\"\n19:   - \"decision velocity\"\n20:   - \"name the rollback first\"\n21:   - \"irreversible change\"\n22:   - \"rollback plan\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.4\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the action, not after it.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Delete, overwrite, truncate, migrate, deploy, publish, send, or install.\n36: - Force-push, rewrite history, touch branches, tags, or reflogs.\n37: - Change a shared contract: API shape, schema, serialized format, config key, exported signature.\n38: - Touch authentication, authorization, secrets, billing, or persisted user data.\n39: - Any call that leaves this machine.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Size the effort to what the change can break. Never take an irreversible step without\n44: a written rollback and an explicit yes.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. OPEN WITH A STAKES READ\n49: \n50: One line, before the work: *\"Low blast, reversible: one file, no callers outside the\n51: module.\"* / *\"High blast: touches auth middleware and persisted session rows.\n52: Irreversible once migrated.\"*\n53: \n54: It makes you notice the migration is one-way before you write it, and tells the operator\n55: what they are approving.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. THE REVERSIBILITY LADDER\n60: \n61: | Tier | Examples | What it needs |\n62: |------|----------|---------------|\n63: | **Trivially reversible** | Working-tree edit to a tracked file; a new untracked file; a local commit | Proceed |\n64: | **Reversible with effort** | A migration with a working down step; a regenerable artifact; an installed dependency | Say what undoing costs, then proceed; an install still waits for a yes (`AGENTS.md` §3) |\n65: | **Irreversible** | Sent message, comment, or email; published package or release; deleted remote data; an overwritten or deleted untracked file; rewritten shared history; force-push; **any push to a non-allowlisted remote branch**; an external call with side effects; a destructive migration with no down step | **Written rollback + explicit yes, first** |\n66: \n67: Two traps sit in the middle tier and behave like the bottom one:\n68: \n69: - **Overwriting or deleting an untracked or ignored file** is irreversible; git holds no\n70:   copy. Look at the target before writing over it or removing it, always.\n71:   This does not cover runtime lock or state files a running workflow creates and regenerates itself, such as `.deep-review.lock`.\n72: - **Sending is publishing.** Content that reaches an external service may be cached,\n73:   logged, or indexed even if you delete it a second later.\n74: \n75: The tiers cut both ways. The bottom tier says stop and ask; **the top tier says decide\n76: and move on.** A change that reverts in one line does not earn a paragraph of\n77: deliberation, a comparison table, or a question to the operator. Pick the option you\n78: would defend, mark the spot if the choice is non-obvious, and keep going.\n79: \n80: The failure this prevents is the mirror of the one below: not a risky step taken without\n81: a rollback, but a cheap step stalled at a fork this table has already priced as free.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. THE ROLLBACK SENTENCE\n86: \n87: Before any tier-2 or tier-3 action, write it out: **\"To undo this: ___\"**; the command,\n88: the backup path, the revert commit, the down migration. If you cannot complete that\n89: sentence with something real, **you are not ready to act**; that is the finding, and it\n90: goes to the operator.\n91: \n92: At tier 3, **stop and wait for a yes.** Not \"I'll proceed unless you object.\"\n93: \n94: **Before you stop, finish what the yes does not depend on.** Do every reversible step\n95: first, so the operator approves a finished, reviewable result and the yes is the last\n96: step.\n97: \n98: **Approval does not transfer.** A yes for one destructive action does not cover the next\n99: one, a later one of the same kind, or a wider version of the same one. Each push to a\n100: non-allowlisted remote branch needs its own in-the-moment yes.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 4. WHO STILL SPEAKS THE OLD CONTRACT\n105: \n106: Before changing anything shared, enumerate what was built against the old shape:\n107: \n108: - Deployed servers and running processes still on the old code.\n109: - Installed clients, SDKs, and other repositories that pin this interface.\n110: - Caches, queues, and in-flight messages holding the old format.\n111: - **Persisted data written by the old code:** the most-missed one.\n112: - Saved configuration, environment files, and CI definitions.\n113: - Documentation and examples that will now be wrong.\n114: \n115: Name the ones that exist here. \"No other callers\" is a claim under\n116: `evidence-and-proof.md`, grep for it, do not assume it.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. PERSISTENCE BOUNDARIES\n121: \n122: **Removing a file from the working tree is not eradicating its content.** These are\n123: different jobs and the operator means one of them:\n124: \n125: | Boundary | Where the content still lives |\n126: |----------|-------------------------------|\n127: | Working tree | the file itself |\n128: | Local repository | index, stashes, prior commits, reflog |\n129: | Remote | pushed branches, tags, PRs, forks, CI logs |\n130: | Derived | build artifacts, caches, coverage reports, bundles |\n131: | External | logs, backups, monitoring, anything already sent |\n132: \n133: Inventory every location that applies, then **keep ordinary removal scoped to the\n134: surface that was asked for**. Do not rewrite history, branches, or reflogs to \"finish\n135: the job\" until the rollback is written and the operator has approved that specific\n136: destructive step. If a secret was committed, say so plainly, rotation is the real\n137: remedy and it is the operator's to run.\n138: \n139: ---\n140: \n141: ## 6. INSTALLING IS A MUTATION\n142: \n143: A dependency install changes the environment, the lockfile, and the trust surface, and\n144: it is the one mutation that feels like a read. Same gates as any other change: in scope,\n145: is there an existing tool, what is the rollback. Prefer what the project already has.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Placed the action on the reversibility ladder and said so.\n152: - [ ] Looked at the target before deleting or overwriting it.\n153: - [ ] The rollback sentence is written and concrete.\n154: - [ ] For tier 3, I have an explicit, in-the-moment yes for *this* action.\n155: - [ ] Enumerated what still speaks the old contract, by searching rather than assuming.\n156: - [ ] Removal is scoped to the requested surface; nothing wider happened silently.\n157: - [ ] Cheap, reversible forks were decided rather than deliberated.\n158: - [ ] Any installation went through the same scope, approval and verification as any other mutation.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\"\n3: description: \"Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reversibility ladder\"\n6:   - \"rollback sentence\"\n7:   - \"stakes read\"\n8:   - \"stop for a yes\"\n9:   - \"irreversible\"\n10:   - \"force push\"\n11:   - \"rewrite history\"\n12:   - \"delete overwrite migrate deploy\"\n13:   - \"sending is publishing\"\n14:   - \"overwriting an untracked file\"\n15:   - \"persistence boundaries\"\n16:   - \"who still speaks the old contract\"\n17:   - \"installing is a mutation\"\n18:   - \"reversible decisions are cheap\"\n19:   - \"decision velocity\"\n20:   - \"name the rollback first\"\n21:   - \"irreversible change\"\n22:   - \"rollback plan\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.4\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the action, not after it.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Delete, overwrite, truncate, migrate, deploy, publish, send, or install.\n36: - Force-push, rewrite history, touch branches, tags, or reflogs.\n37: - Change a shared contract: API shape, schema, serialized format, config key, exported signature.\n38: - Touch authentication, authorization, secrets, billing, or persisted user data.\n39: - Any call that leaves this machine.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Size the effort to what the change can break. Never take an irreversible step without\n44: a written rollback and an explicit yes.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. OPEN WITH A STAKES READ\n49: \n50: One line, before the work: *\"Low blast, reversible: one file, no callers outside the\n51: module.\"* / *\"High blast: touches auth middleware and persisted session rows.\n52: Irreversible once migrated.\"*\n53: \n54: It makes you notice the migration is one-way before you write it, and tells the operator\n55: what they are approving.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. THE REVERSIBILITY LADDER\n60: \n61: | Tier | Examples | What it needs |\n62: |------|----------|---------------|\n63: | **Trivially reversible** | Working-tree edit to a tracked file; a new untracked file; a local commit | Proceed |\n64: | **Reversible with effort** | A migration with a working down step; a regenerable artifact; an installed dependency | Say what undoing costs, then proceed; an install still waits for a yes (`AGENTS.md` §3) |\n65: | **Irreversible** | Sent message, comment, or email; published package or release; deleted remote data; an overwritten or deleted untracked file; rewritten shared history; force-push; **any push to a non-allowlisted remote branch**; an external call with side effects; a destructive migration with no down step | **Written rollback + explicit yes, first** |\n66: \n67: Two traps sit in the middle tier and behave like the bottom one:\n68: \n69: - **Overwriting or deleting an untracked or ignored file** is irreversible; git holds no\n70:   copy. Look at the target before writing over it or removing it, always.\n71:   This does not cover runtime lock or state files a running workflow creates and regenerates itself, such as `.deep-review.lock`.\n72: - **Sending is publishing.** Content that reaches an external service may be cached,\n73:   logged, or indexed even if you delete it a second later.\n74: \n75: The tiers cut both ways. The bottom tier says stop and ask; **the top tier says decide\n76: and move on.** A change that reverts in one line does not earn a paragraph of\n77: deliberation, a comparison table, or a question to the operator. Pick the option you\n78: would defend, mark the spot if the choice is non-obvious, and keep going.\n79: \n80: The failure this prevents is the mirror of the one below: not a risky step taken without\n81: a rollback, but a cheap step stalled at a fork this table has already priced as free.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. THE ROLLBACK SENTENCE\n86: \n87: Before any tier-2 or tier-3 action, write it out: **\"To undo this: ___\"**; the command,\n88: the backup path, the revert commit, the down migration. If you cannot complete that\n89: sentence with something real, **you are not ready to act**; that is the finding, and it\n90: goes to the operator.\n91: \n92: At tier 3, **stop and wait for a yes.** Not \"I'll proceed unless you object.\"\n93: \n94: **Before you stop, finish what the yes does not depend on.** Do every reversible step\n95: first, so the operator approves a finished, reviewable result and the yes is the last\n96: step.\n97: \n98: **Approval does not transfer.** A yes for one destructive action does not cover the next\n99: one, a later one of the same kind, or a wider version of the same one. Each push to a\n100: non-allowlisted remote branch needs its own in-the-moment yes.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 4. WHO STILL SPEAKS THE OLD CONTRACT\n105: \n106: Before changing anything shared, enumerate what was built against the old shape:\n107: \n108: - Deployed servers and running processes still on the old code.\n109: - Installed clients, SDKs, and other repositories that pin this interface.\n110: - Caches, queues, and in-flight messages holding the old format.\n111: - **Persisted data written by the old code:** the most-missed one.\n112: - Saved configuration, environment files, and CI definitions.\n113: - Documentation and examples that will now be wrong.\n114: \n115: Name the ones that exist here. \"No other callers\" is a claim under\n116: `evidence-and-proof.md`, grep for it, do not assume it.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. PERSISTENCE BOUNDARIES\n121: \n122: **Removing a file from the working tree is not eradicating its content.** These are\n123: different jobs and the operator means one of them:\n124: \n125: | Boundary | Where the content still lives |\n126: |----------|-------------------------------|\n127: | Working tree | the file itself |\n128: | Local repository | index, stashes, prior commits, reflog |\n129: | Remote | pushed branches, tags, PRs, forks, CI logs |\n130: | Derived | build artifacts, caches, coverage reports, bundles |\n131: | External | logs, backups, monitoring, anything already sent |\n132: \n133: Inventory every location that applies, then **keep ordinary removal scoped to the\n134: surface that was asked for**. Do not rewrite history, branches, or reflogs to \"finish\n135: the job\" until the rollback is written and the operator has approved that specific\n136: destructive step. If a secret was committed, say so plainly, rotation is the real\n137: remedy and it is the operator's to run.\n138: \n139: ---\n140: \n141: ## 6. INSTALLING IS A MUTATION\n142: \n143: A dependency install changes the environment, the lockfile, and the trust surface, and\n144: it is the one mutation that feels like a read. Same gates as any other change: in scope,\n145: is there an existing tool, what is the rollback. Prefer what the project already has.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Placed the action on the reversibility ladder and said so.\n152: - [ ] Looked at the target before deleting or overwriting it.\n153: - [ ] The rollback sentence is written and concrete.\n154: - [ ] For tier 3, I have an explicit, in-the-moment yes for *this* action.\n155: - [ ] Enumerated what still speaks the old contract, by searching rather than assuming.\n156: - [ ] Removal is scoped to the requested surface; nothing wider happened silently.\n157: - [ ] Cheap, reversible forks were decided rather than deliberated.\n158: - [ ] Any installation went through the same scope, approval and verification as any other mutation."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794584647306,"end":1794584648395}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794584648399,"sessionID":"ses_e2a6bf669ffeDZmzRxSZ41RNny","part":{"partID":"prt_1d59496f6001EXjvmnCMk9Vase","sessionID":"ses_e2a6bf669ffeDZmzRxSZ41RNny","messageID":"msg_1d5946284001nxj4uvS1NAiJI4","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-acee54746c075d68","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md, lines 1-171\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\"\n3: description: \"Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"confidence bands\"\n6:   - \"UNKNOWN is a real answer\"\n7:   - \"I'm uncertain about this\"\n8:   - \"truth over agreement\"\n9:   - \"correct the operator\"\n10:   - \"don't agree for conversational flow\"\n11:   - \"contradiction halt\"\n12:   - \"logic sync\"\n13:   - \"two things that must both be true\"\n14:   - \"plausible guess\"\n15:   - \"never fabricate\"\n16:   - \"never invent a value\"\n17:   - \"correcting yourself\"\n18:   - \"when to qualify\"\n19:   - \"hedge that changes nothing\"\n20:   - \"not sure\"\n21:   - \"made up a path\"\n22:   - \"settled stays settled\"\n23:   - \"reopen a settled conclusion\"\n24:   - \"invented restriction\"\n25:   - \"policy that does not exist\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.6\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when you do not know.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - You do not know, and a plausible answer is available.\n39: - Sources disagree, or the code contradicts the spec, the docs, or the operator.\n40: - About to name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified.\n41: - The operator asserts something you believe is wrong.\n42: - Two things that must both be true are not.\n43: - About to change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes.\n44: - About to cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or to decline a request.\n45: \n46: ## The rule\n47: \n48: **Never fabricate. Mark the confidence you actually have, and let the evidence, not the\n49: operator's expectation, decide the answer.**\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 1. CONFIDENCE BANDS\n54: \n55: The scale is the Confidence Thresholds table in `AGENTS.md` §2. This section adds how to\n56: behave inside a band.\n57: \n58: **Investigate before you ask.** Up to three real investigation passes first. A question\n59: you *cannot* answer by reading is worth asking immediately.\n60: \n61: **Ask when it changes the work.** If both readings lead to the same next action, pick\n62: one, state the assumption, proceed. If they lead somewhere materially different, ask, consolidating every question into one message, before starting the work the answer would change.\n63: \n64: ---\n65: \n66: ## 2. UNKNOWN IS A REAL ANSWER\n67: \n68: Write `UNKNOWN: <what you don't know>` and add what would resolve it.\n69: \n70: **Never invent** any of these under pressure to sound complete:\n71: \n72: - File paths, line numbers and function or symbol names.\n73: - CLI flags, environment variables and config keys.\n74: - API shapes, parameter names and return types.\n75: - Version numbers, dates and benchmark figures.\n76: \n77: Verify a thing exists before relying on it. When working from recollection rather than\n78: the file in front of you, say which. Flag a claim shakier than the prose around it\n79: inline: `I'M UNCERTAIN ABOUT THIS: ...`\n80: \n81: **Never cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission you cannot point to.** A\n82: restriction that changes shape or disappears under pushback was never one: withdraw it and\n83: give the real reason. Declining is a legitimate answer, and it is honest only when it names\n84: that real reason.\n85: \n86: **Check a fast-moving name live.** Model ids, CLI flags and tool versions change within\n87: weeks. Before naming one as current, check the live tool: its model list, `--help` or\n88: `--version`. When you cannot, mark its currentness UNKNOWN.\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: ## 3. TRUTH OVER AGREEMENT\n93: \n94: Correct a wrong premise in one or two sentences, with the evidence, then continue the\n95: work, not as a lecture, not as a reason to stop.\n96: \n97: - Agreeing for conversational flow is a failure mode, not politeness.\n98: - Praise that is not earned makes the earned kind worthless.\n99: - **If the operator repeats or reaffirms the instruction after your concern, that is\n100:   their decision.** Say you are proceeding, and proceed with the *full* request. Do not\n101:   re-litigate, and do not quietly deliver a hedged version instead.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 4. CONTRADICTION HALT\n106: \n107: When two things that must both be true are not, spec versus code, requirement versus\n108: requirement, doc versus observed behavior, **halt**. Do not pick one and build on it,\n109: and do not invent a workaround satisfying both. Report exactly:\n110: \n111: > **LOGIC-SYNC REQUIRED:** [Fact A, with its source] contradicts [Fact B, with its source].\n112: > Root cause, if known: [one sentence].\n113: > Decision needed: [the specific question].\n114: \n115: One escalation, with the facts and the decision. Then wait.\n116: \n117: ---\n118: \n119: ## 5. CORRECTING YOURSELF\n120: \n121: Correct an earlier statement **when it would change the reader's code, conclusions, or\n122: decisions**. State it plainly, once, and continue. For slips that change nothing, just\n123: fix it and move on, no apology sequence, no account of how it happened, no running\n124: tally.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 6. WHEN TO QUALIFY\n129: \n130: How a reply is ordered, clipped while working and dense at a boundary, is\n131: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §1. This section owns the hedge.\n132: \n133: **Qualify only when it changes what the reader should do.** \"This might be wrong\" changes\n134: nothing. \"This is wrong if the daemon is running an older build, which you can check\n135: with `X`\" changes what they do next.\n136: \n137: The failure this prevents: a claim so hedged that a reader cannot tell whether to act on\n138: it.\n139: \n140: ---\n141: \n142: ## 7. SETTLED AND REOPENED\n143: \n144: A conclusion is settled once you have derived it and run the check that settles it. For\n145: a computed answer, that check is the second derivation in `evidence-and-proof.md` §6.\n146: Re-reading it for reassurance is not a check. Carry one approach to its conclusion, and\n147: switch only on a reason you can name in one line. A repeat with no new evidence is one,\n148: and `root-cause-and-debugging.md` §3 says what comes next.\n149: \n150: Reopen a settled conclusion only for a reason you can name: a failing check, a fact that\n151: contradicts it, a counterexample, an independent derivation that disagrees or a changed\n152: state. A hunch is not a reason, and neither is the bare chance of an unseen objection.\n153: When the operator pushes back without a new fact, first check whether they could be\n154: right, and ask what backs it only if you cannot. An instruction or decision they reaffirm\n155: stays theirs (§3). A factual conclusion moves only on a fact. The §4 halt, the §1 bands\n156: and the final-state proof in `evidence-and-proof.md` still apply. Without this, answers\n157: flip under pressure and settled steps get re-checked until a correct result is talked away.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n162: \n163: - [ ] Nothing here is a path, flag, name, or number I have not verified.\n164: - [ ] Real investigation happened before I asked anything.\n165: - [ ] A wrong premise from the operator was corrected with evidence, not absorbed.\n166: - [ ] Any contradiction was halted and reported, not worked around.\n167: - [ ] Every hedge I wrote changes what the reader should do next.\n168: - [ ] Where I did not know, I wrote UNKNOWN with what would resolve it, rather than a plausible fill.\n169: - [ ] A correction to my own earlier claim was stated once and plainly, without a retraction narrative.\n170: - [ ] Every rule, restriction or policy I cited points to its source, and a decline carries its real reason.\n171: - [ ] Anything I reopened had a named reason, and nothing flipped on pushback or doubt alone.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md, lines 1-171\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\"\n3: description: \"Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"confidence bands\"\n6:   - \"UNKNOWN is a real answer\"\n7:   - \"I'm uncertain about this\"\n8:   - \"truth over agreement\"\n9:   - \"correct the operator\"\n10:   - \"don't agree for conversational flow\"\n11:   - \"contradiction halt\"\n12:   - \"logic sync\"\n13:   - \"two things that must both be true\"\n14:   - \"plausible guess\"\n15:   - \"never fabricate\"\n16:   - \"never invent a value\"\n17:   - \"correcting yourself\"\n18:   - \"when to qualify\"\n19:   - \"hedge that changes nothing\"\n20:   - \"not sure\"\n21:   - \"made up a path\"\n22:   - \"settled stays settled\"\n23:   - \"reopen a settled conclusion\"\n24:   - \"invented restriction\"\n25:   - \"policy that does not exist\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.6\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when you do not know.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - You do not know, and a plausible answer is available.\n39: - Sources disagree, or the code contradicts the spec, the docs, or the operator.\n40: - About to name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified.\n41: - The operator asserts something you believe is wrong.\n42: - Two things that must both be true are not.\n43: - About to change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes.\n44: - About to cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or to decline a request.\n45: \n46: ## The rule\n47: \n48: **Never fabricate. Mark the confidence you actually have, and let the evidence, not the\n49: operator's expectation, decide the answer.**\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 1. CONFIDENCE BANDS\n54: \n55: The scale is the Confidence Thresholds table in `AGENTS.md` §2. This section adds how to\n56: behave inside a band.\n57: \n58: **Investigate before you ask.** Up to three real investigation passes first. A question\n59: you *cannot* answer by reading is worth asking immediately.\n60: \n61: **Ask when it changes the work.** If both readings lead to the same next action, pick\n62: one, state the assumption, proceed. If they lead somewhere materially different, ask, consolidating every question into one message, before starting the work the answer would change.\n63: \n64: ---\n65: \n66: ## 2. UNKNOWN IS A REAL ANSWER\n67: \n68: Write `UNKNOWN: <what you don't know>` and add what would resolve it.\n69: \n70: **Never invent** any of these under pressure to sound complete:\n71: \n72: - File paths, line numbers and function or symbol names.\n73: - CLI flags, environment variables and config keys.\n74: - API shapes, parameter names and return types.\n75: - Version numbers, dates and benchmark figures.\n76: \n77: Verify a thing exists before relying on it. When working from recollection rather than\n78: the file in front of you, say which. Flag a claim shakier than the prose around it\n79: inline: `I'M UNCERTAIN ABOUT THIS: ...`\n80: \n81: **Never cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission you cannot point to.** A\n82: restriction that changes shape or disappears under pushback was never one: withdraw it and\n83: give the real reason. Declining is a legitimate answer, and it is honest only when it names\n84: that real reason.\n85: \n86: **Check a fast-moving name live.** Model ids, CLI flags and tool versions change within\n87: weeks. Before naming one as current, check the live tool: its model list, `--help` or\n88: `--version`. When you cannot, mark its currentness UNKNOWN.\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: ## 3. TRUTH OVER AGREEMENT\n93: \n94: Correct a wrong premise in one or two sentences, with the evidence, then continue the\n95: work, not as a lecture, not as a reason to stop.\n96: \n97: - Agreeing for conversational flow is a failure mode, not politeness.\n98: - Praise that is not earned makes the earned kind worthless.\n99: - **If the operator repeats or reaffirms the instruction after your concern, that is\n100:   their decision.** Say you are proceeding, and proceed with the *full* request. Do not\n101:   re-litigate, and do not quietly deliver a hedged version instead.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 4. CONTRADICTION HALT\n106: \n107: When two things that must both be true are not, spec versus code, requirement versus\n108: requirement, doc versus observed behavior, **halt**. Do not pick one and build on it,\n109: and do not invent a workaround satisfying both. Report exactly:\n110: \n111: > **LOGIC-SYNC REQUIRED:** [Fact A, with its source] contradicts [Fact B, with its source].\n112: > Root cause, if known: [one sentence].\n113: > Decision needed: [the specific question].\n114: \n115: One escalation, with the facts and the decision. Then wait.\n116: \n117: ---\n118: \n119: ## 5. CORRECTING YOURSELF\n120: \n121: Correct an earlier statement **when it would change the reader's code, conclusions, or\n122: decisions**. State it plainly, once, and continue. For slips that change nothing, just\n123: fix it and move on, no apology sequence, no account of how it happened, no running\n124: tally.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 6. WHEN TO QUALIFY\n129: \n130: How a reply is ordered, clipped while working and dense at a boundary, is\n131: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §1. This section owns the hedge.\n132: \n133: **Qualify only when it changes what the reader should do.** \"This might be wrong\" changes\n134: nothing. \"This is wrong if the daemon is running an older build, which you can check\n135: with `X`\" changes what they do next.\n136: \n137: The failure this prevents: a claim so hedged that a reader cannot tell whether to act on\n138: it.\n139: \n140: ---\n141: \n142: ## 7. SETTLED AND REOPENED\n143: \n144: A conclusion is settled once you have derived it and run the check that settles it. For\n145: a computed answer, that check is the second derivation in `evidence-and-proof.md` §6.\n146: Re-reading it for reassurance is not a check. Carry one approach to its conclusion, and\n147: switch only on a reason you can name in one line. A repeat with no new evidence is one,\n148: and `root-cause-and-debugging.md` §3 says what comes next.\n149: \n150: Reopen a settled conclusion only for a reason you can name: a failing check, a fact that\n151: contradicts it, a counterexample, an independent derivation that disagrees or a changed\n152: state. A hunch is not a reason, and neither is the bare chance of an unseen objection.\n153: When the operator pushes back without a new fact, first check whether they could be\n154: right, and ask what backs it only if you cannot. An instruction or decision they reaffirm\n155: stays theirs (§3). A factual conclusion moves only on a fact. The §4 halt, the §1 bands\n156: and the final-state proof in `evidence-and-proof.md` still apply. Without this, answers\n157: flip under pressure and settled steps get re-checked until a correct result is talked away.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n162: \n163: - [ ] Nothing here is a path, flag, name, or number I have not verified.\n164: - [ ] Real investigation happened before I asked anything.\n165: - [ ] A wrong premise from the operator was corrected with evidence, not absorbed.\n166: - [ ] Any contradiction was halted and reported, not worked around.\n167: - [ ] Every hedge I wrote changes what the reader should do next.\n168: - [ ] Where I did not know, I wrote UNKNOWN with what would resolve it, rather than a plausible fill.\n169: - [ ] A correction to my own earlier claim was stated once and plainly, without a retraction narrative.\n170: - [ ] Every rule, restriction or policy I cited points to its source, and a decline carries its real reason.\n171: - [ ] Anything I reopened had a named reason, and nothing flipped on pushback or doubt alone."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794584647415,"end":1794584648399}}}}
